### Order To Cash — the Gateway (feature 25)
###
### THE FIRST FILE HERE THAT CALLS THE BUSINESS ITSELF. Every other file in
### this folder probes infrastructure, because until the Gateway landed the
### interesting operations were NATS request-reply and the REST Client
### extension speaks HTTP only. That is no longer true: the Gateway is the
### single public entry point, and it translates HTTP into the NATS RPC calls
### the services answer.
###
### Requires: pnpm dc:up:infra, then the services this file's requests need:
###   pnpm dev:orders  dev:fulfillment  dev:billing  dev:projector
###   pnpm --filter @otc/gateway build && pnpm --filter @otc/gateway start
###
### Read endpoints (GET /orders, /invoices, ...) need only the PROJECTOR and
### MongoDB — they are served exclusively from the read model (R54), never by
### reading a write database. Write endpoints (POST /orders, ...) need the
### service that answers the RPC, and will return 503 naming the subject if it
### is down — an instant, honest failure rather than a timeout.

@gateway  = http://localhost:3001

### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 1. Authentication
### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### A single operator identity — deliberately not multi-tenant
### (domain-model.md §9). Credentials come from .env:
### GATEWAY_OPERATOR_USERNAME / GATEWAY_OPERATOR_PASSWORD.
###
### Send THIS request first. `# @name login` captures the response so every
### request below can reuse the token without you copying anything.

# @name login
POST {{gateway}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "operator",
  "password": "otc_operator_dev_password_change_me"
}

###
### Reply: { "accessToken": "...", "tokenType": "Bearer", "expiresIn": 3600 }
### A wrong password returns 401 INVALID_CREDENTIALS as application/problem+json.

@token = {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

### Who am I — the cheapest proof the token works
GET {{gateway}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Without a token: 401. Exactly four routes are public — POST /auth/login,
### GET /health/live, GET /health/ready and GET /docs — and a test asserts
### that every OTHER registered route rejects an anonymous request.
GET {{gateway}}/auth/me

### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 2. Health and documentation — the four public routes
### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Liveness — is the process up
GET {{gateway}}/health/live

### Readiness — real dependency indicators arrive with feature 27
GET {{gateway}}/health/ready

### Swagger UI, serving specs/shared/openapi.yaml verbatim.
### Open http://localhost:3001/docs in a browser for the interactive version.
GET {{gateway}}/docs

### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 3. Placing an order — the saga's front door
### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### Returns 201 with Location and X-Correlation-Id. The correlation id IS the
### order id (saga.md makes that an invariant), and every fact in this order's
### saga carries it.
###
### The saga then runs ASYNCHRONOUSLY: stock reserved, credit approved, order
### confirmed, despatched, invoiced. Give it a second, then read it back at
### section 4 or watch it live at section 8.

# @name placeOrder
POST {{gateway}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "retailerCode": "CarrefourEs",
  "companyCode": "IBERFOODS",
  "currency": "EUR",
  "lines": [
    { "productCode": "PRD-0001", "quantity": 2 }
  ]
}

###
@orderId = {{placeOrder.response.body.orderId}}

### A total ending in .99 — the credit simulator REJECTS it and the saga
### compensates: stock released, order cancelled, reason simulated_cents_rule.
### `unitPrice` is supplied explicitly to engineer the total (R42).
POST {{gateway}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "retailerCode": "CarrefourEs",
  "companyCode": "IBERFOODS",
  "currency": "EUR",
  "lines": [
    { "productCode": "PRD-0001", "quantity": 1, "unitPrice": 24999 }
  ]
}

### A GENUINE credit rejection — over the buyer's limit. Produces the identical
### fact and the identical compensation as the simulated one above, differing
### only in `reason: over_limit`. That equivalence is requirement R44.
POST {{gateway}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "retailerCode": "CarrefourEs",
  "companyCode": "IBERFOODS",
  "currency": "EUR",
  "lines": [
    { "productCode": "PRD-0001", "quantity": 21 }
  ]
}

### An order with no lines — 400, rejected by the domain (invariant O1, R5)
POST {{gateway}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "retailerCode": "CarrefourEs",
  "companyCode": "IBERFOODS",
  "currency": "EUR",
  "lines": []
}

### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 4. Reading orders — from the read model ONLY (R54)
### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### These need the projector running, not the write services. A guard proves
### mysql2 and drizzle-orm cannot even be RESOLVED from the gateway process,
### so a write-model read is structurally impossible rather than merely absent.

### The order just placed, with its full saga timeline ordered by occurredAt
GET {{gateway}}/orders/{{orderId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 202 vs 404 — worth understanding, it was review finding F3.
### An id THIS gateway process issued, not yet projected, returns 202 with
### `projection_pending` and Retry-After. An id it never issued returns 404,
### because openapi.yaml says "a genuine 404 means the identifier is unknown".
### The window is bounded and TTL'd (5 min default) and PROCESS-LOCAL: with two
### replicas, a POST on one and a GET on the other returns 404.
GET {{gateway}}/orders/00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### First page of orders, newest first
GET {{gateway}}/orders?page=1&pageSize=20
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Only the cancelled ones — both compensation paths land here
GET {{gateway}}/orders?status=cancelled&page=1&pageSize=20
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Repeat `status` to accept several
GET {{gateway}}/orders?status=invoiced&status=completed&page=1&pageSize=20
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### By business reference
GET {{gateway}}/orders?orderReference=ORD-000001
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### By trading partners
GET {{gateway}}/orders?retailerCode=CarrefourEs&companyCode=IBERFOODS
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Cancel an order — an operator note lands on the timeline
POST {{gateway}}/orders/{{orderId}}/cancel
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "note": "Cancelled by hand from gateway.http"
}

### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 5. Invoices and remittance — closing the order-to-cash cycle
### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Everything still awaiting payment
GET {{gateway}}/invoices?status=issued&page=1&pageSize=20
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Issued more than N minutes ago — what a payment robot would poll for
GET {{gateway}}/invoices?status=issued&issuedBeforeMinutes=5
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### REGISTER A PAYMENT. Take an `invoiceId` from the list above and paste it in.
###
### This endpoint closes a gap recorded at feature 22: Billing needs the ORDER
### id as its correlation id, but an HTTP caller holds only an invoice id. The
### gateway resolves invoice -> order through the READ MODEL, whose _id IS the
### order id — never by reading a write database.
###
### `paymentReference` is the idempotency key (R48): send this twice and the
### second returns `duplicate` with the same paidAt, not a second payment.
### `amount` must equal the invoice totalAmount and currency EXACTLY —
### partial and over-payment are out of scope (invariant B10, R49).

@invoiceId = 75ad5d4b-6946-4373-a9c7-ca28895a0189
#"invoiceReference": "INV-000009"

POST {{gateway}}/invoices/{{invoiceId}}/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "paymentReference": "PAY-HTTP-0001",
  "amount": { "amount": 49998, "currency": "EUR" },
  "valueDate": "2026-08-25T12:00:00.000Z",
  "source": "operator"
}

### Send the SAME request again — R48 idempotency, `outcome: duplicate`,
### byte-identical paidAt. That equality is what proves the original record was
### returned rather than a second payment written.

### A deliberately wrong amount — rejected, no fact emitted, ledger untouched
POST {{gateway}}/invoices/{{invoiceId}}/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "paymentReference": "PAY-HTTP-WRONG",
  "amount": { "amount": 1, "currency": "EUR" },
  "valueDate": "2026-08-25T12:00:00.000Z",
  "source": "test"
}

