POST {{gateway}}/stock/replenish
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "companyCode": "IBERFOODS",
  "lines": [
    { "productCode": "PRD-0001", "units": 100 }
  ]
}

### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 7. Credit and catalogue
### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Buyer credit lines — limit, exposure, available.
### Exposure is RECOMPUTED from the ledger on every load, never stored, so it
### cannot drift from the hold/release entries that produced it.
GET {{gateway}}/credits?page=1&pageSize=20
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### One buyer's credit
GET {{gateway}}/credits?retailerCode=CarrefourEs&companyCode=IBERFOODS
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Catalogue — the reference data every other request's codes come from
GET {{gateway}}/catalog/products
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

###
GET {{gateway}}/catalog/retailers
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

###
GET {{gateway}}/catalog/companies
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 8. The SSE stream — live saga progress
### ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### CAVEAT, and the reason this section is last: the REST Client extension
### does not render text/event-stream incrementally. It will hold the request
### open and show nothing useful. This block documents the contract; use curl
### below to actually watch it.
###
### Fed by the projector's NATS update signal (R55) — a NATS core publish, not
### a Kafka topic, chosen at the gate because a shared Kafka consumer group
### would deliver each signal to exactly ONE gateway replica.

GET {{gateway}}/orders/stream
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Accept: text/event-stream

###
### Watch it properly, in a terminal:
###
###   TOKEN=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:3001/auth/login \
###     -H 'content-type: application/json' \
###     -d '{"username":"operator","password":"otc_operator_dev_password_change_me"}' \
###     | node -pe 'JSON.parse(require("fs").readFileSync(0)).accessToken')
###
###   curl -N -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
###     http://localhost:3001/orders/stream
###
### Then place an order in another terminal (section 3, or `pnpm order:place`)
### and watch the frames arrive.
###
### Frames look like:
###   id: 1755511234567-17
###   event: order.updated
###   data: {"orderId":"...","status":"confirmed", ...}
###
### `ping` and `stream.ready` carry NO `id:` line — deliberately. A browser's
### EventSource updates its remembered Last-Event-ID from ANY id-bearing frame,
### heartbeat or not. `ping` fires every 15s while real content is rare, so if
### it carried an id, a client's remembered cursor would almost always be a
### heartbeat's — which is never in the replay buffer (nothing to replay) — and
### reconnecting would answer `resumed: false` on nearly every disconnect, not
### just when the buffer genuinely aged out. Found by hand-testing this file:
### reconnecting with a copied PING id gave resumed:false; the same reconnect
### with a copied `timeline.appended` id gave resumed:true, as it should.
###
### On reconnect a browser's EventSource sends Last-Event-ID automatically and
### the stream resumes after that cursor from a BOUNDED buffer. Away longer
### than the buffer holds, it answers `stream.ready` with `resumed: false` and
### the client must re-fetch — stated honestly rather than hidden, because the
### stream is a notification channel and the read model is the source of truth.
### Delivery is at-least-once, so clients deduplicate on the eventId in `data`.
###
### NOTE: EventSource cannot set an Authorization header, which is why the web
### app (feature 26 onward) needs a different scheme — carried as review
### finding F14.

### Follow one order only
GET {{gateway}}/orders/stream?orderId={{orderId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Accept: text/event-stream
